{"causes":["The Persian Wars (499-449 BC) created a power vacuum in Persia, leading to a series of conflicts between Greek city-states and the Persian Empire.","The conquests of Cyrus the Younger (401-401 BC) sparked a wave of Greek mercenaries seeking new opportunities in the ancient world.","The rise of Xenophon (c. 430-354 BC) as a prominent ancient Greek historian and military leader set the stage for his account of the expedition"],"effects":["The expedition of the Ten Thousand influenced the development of Western literature, with Xenophon's account of the journey serving as a model for later historical accounts and literary works.","The legacy of the expedition can be seen in the cultural and intellectual exchanges between ancient Greece and Persia, reflecting the complex and dynamic nature of ancient world history.","The survival of the Greek mercenaries against incredible odds reflected the resilience and adaptability of these ancient warriors, who played a significant role in shaping the history of the ancient world"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Xenophon","sourceName":"Xenophon","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Anabasis","targetName":"Anabasis","context":"Xenophon's account of the expedition"},{"sourceSlug":"Cyrus the Younger","sourceName":"Cyrus the Younger","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Ten Thousand","targetName":"Ten Thousand","context":"Cyrus the Younger hired the Greek mercenaries to help him seize the throne of Persia"},{"sourceSlug":"Artaxerxes II","sourceName":"Artaxerxes II","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Persian Empire","targetName":"Persian Empire","context":"Artaxerxes II was the king of Persia who faced a rebellion led by Cyrus the Younger"},{"sourceSlug":"Xenophon","sourceName":"Xenophon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Greek mercenaries","targetName":"Greek mercenaries","context":"Xenophon's account of the expedition reflected the experiences of the Greek mercenaries"},{"sourceSlug":"Persian Empire","sourceName":"Persian Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Anabasis","targetName":"Anabasis","context":"The Persian Empire was the context in which the expedition of the Ten Thousand took place"},{"sourceSlug":"Greek city-states","sourceName":"Greek city-states","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Persian Wars","targetName":"Persian Wars","context":"The Greek city-states were involved in the Persian Wars, which created a power vacuum in Persia"},{"sourceSlug":"Xenophon","sourceName":"Xenophon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Western literature","targetName":"Western literature","context":"Xenophon's account of the expedition influenced the development of Western literature"},{"sourceSlug":"Persia","sourceName":"Persia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Cultural exchange","targetName":"Cultural exchange","context":"The expedition of the Ten Thousand facilitated cultural and intellectual exchanges between ancient Greece and Persia"}],"places":[{"name":"Cunaxa","role":"Battle site"},{"name":"Persia","role":"Expedition route"},{"name":"Greece","role":"Starting point"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Anabasis","type":"Reference article","slug":"anabasis"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q73112","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anabasis_(Xenophon)"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Anabasis contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Anabasis (Greek: Ἀνάβασις, 'The March Upcountry' or 'The Expedition') is the narrative written by the Athenian historian and soldier Xenophon (c. 430–354 BCE), describing the doomed expedition of the Ten Thousand — a Greek mercenary army hired by Cyrus the Younger to seize the Persian throne from his brother Artaxerxes II — and the remarkable 1,500-mile retreat of the survivors through hostile territory in Asia Minor after Cyrus's death at the Battle of Cunaxa (401 BCE). It is one of the greatest adventure narratives of antiquity, a foundational text of Greek prose literature, and a military classic that directly influenced Alexander the Great's Persian campaigns and, through the centuries, countless subsequent military expeditions.

After Cyrus was killed at Cunaxa and the Greek generals treacherously murdered by the Persians, the Ten Thousand found themselves stranded deep in Persia, surrounded by enemies, without leadership, supply lines, or guides. The soldiers elected new leaders (including Xenophon himself, despite being only a civilian volunteer), and under constant harassment by Persian forces, Kardouchian mountain peoples, and harsh terrain and weather, they fought their way northward to the Black Sea coast — the famous cry 'Thalatta! Thalatta!' ('The sea! The sea!') when the first men reached the crest and saw the Black Sea below them is one of the most celebrated moments in ancient literature.

The Anabasis's military significance is exceptional: Alexander the Great reportedly kept a copy annotated by Aristotle and used the expedition as proof that Greek armies could penetrate the Persian heartland. Napoleon cited it as a model of military leadership under adversity. It also provided the earliest sustained Greek ethnographic account of the peoples of Anatolia, Armenia, and the Black Sea coast, influencing Herodotean geography. Xenophon's account of democratic military decision-making — the soldiers voting on strategy, selecting leaders by merit — is also a remarkable document of Greek democratic practices in extremis.","subjects":["Ancient Greece","Classical Era","Military History","Persian Empire","Greek Literature","Ancient History","Xenophon","10 Thousand"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The expedition of the Ten Thousand, as chronicled in Xenophon's Anabasis, had a lasting impact on the development of Western literature and military strategy, reflecting the cultural and intellectual exchanges between ancient Greece and Persia.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
